{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869304,"nonce":"Bir/4WLwEM7UwG3WGtWnzdMrzgE=","message":"Feedbax should expose a machine-readable provider contract so Mandible, Studio, scripts,\nand agents can discover capabilities and validate work before execution.\n\n\nA provider manifest should describe identity, health, schemas, capabilities, artifacts,\nand UI entry points:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"provider\": \"feedbax\",\n  \"kind\": \"experiment_provider\",\n  \"version\": \"feedbax-provider.v1\",\n  \"feedbax_version\": \"0.1.2\",\n  \"health\": {\n    \"check\": \"python:feedbax.provider.health\"\n  },\n  \"capabilities\": {\n    \"open_project\": {\"input_schema\": \"FeedbaxProjectRef\"},\n    \"validate_graph_spec\": {\"input_schema\": \"GraphSpec\"},\n    \"validate_training_spec\": {\"input_schema\": \"TrainingRunSpec\"},\n    \"start_training_run\": {\n      \"input_schema\": \"TrainingRunSpec\",\n      \"output_schema\": \"TrainingRunManifest\",\n      \"requires_review\": true\n    },\n    \"start_evaluation_run\": {\n      \"input_schema\": \"EvaluationRunSpec\",\n      \"output_schema\": \"EvaluationRunManifest\"\n    },\n    \"run_analysis\": {\n      \"input_schema\": \"AnalysisRunSpec\",\n      \"output_schema\": \"AnalysisRunManifest\"\n    },\n    \"publish_report\": {\n      \"input_schema\": \"ReportSpec\",\n      \"output_schema\": \"ReportManifest\"\n    },\n    \"list_components\": {\"output_schema\": \"ComponentRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"validate_component_package\": {\"input_schema\": \"ComponentPackageSpec\"},\n    \"list_tasks\": {\"output_schema\": \"TaskRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"list_losses\": {\"output_schema\": \"LossRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"list_protocols\": {\"output_schema\": \"ProtocolRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"list_analyses\": {\"output_schema\": \"AnalysisRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"validate_analysis_package\": {\"input_schema\": \"AnalysisPackageSpec\"},\n    \"list_metrics\": {\"output_schema\": \"MetricRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"list_renderers\": {\"output_schema\": \"RendererRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"list_artifact_codecs\": {\"output_schema\": \"ArtifactCodecRegistrySnapshot\"},\n    \"list_importers\": {\"output_schema\": \"ImporterRegistrySnapshot\"}\n  },\n  \"artifact_roles\": [\n    \"training_checkpoint\",\n    \"training_history\",\n    \"trajectory_dataset\",\n    \"evaluation_result\",\n    \"analysis_table\",\n    \"figure\",\n    \"report\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis manifest can initially be emitted by a Python function or CLI command. Later it can\nalso be served through Studio HTTP endpoints or exposed through an MCP tool.\n\n\nTransport should be staged rather than settled prematurely:\n\n1. Python API: easiest for tests and local scripts.\n2. CLI: stable for Mandible subprocess calls and human debugging.\n3. HTTP from Studio/worker: useful when Studio is already running.\n4. MCP-style tools: useful for agent interaction once the contract is stable.\n\nThe same schemas and capability names should be reused across transports.\n\n\nFeedbax should validate:\n\n- Graph specs.\n- Training/evaluation/analysis/report specs.\n- Component references and parameter schemas.\n- Artifact references and required artifact roles.\n- Compatibility between a spec and available components/backends.\n\nValidation should not launch compute or mutate project state. It should return structured\nerrors and warnings that Mandible or Studio can display and agents can correct.\n\n\nExecution capabilities should produce manifests and artifacts. An execution response\nshould include:\n\n- Run status or job ID.\n- Manifest path or manifest object.\n- Artifact references for produced files.\n- Logs or log artifact references.\n- Provenance edges to input specs and parent artifacts.\n\nLong-running execution can remain asynchronous. The contract only requires that final\noutputs are recorded as manifests and artifact references.\n\n\nThe component registry is a Feedbax-owned discovery and contract layer. It should not be\nunderstood as merely a folder of generated Python modules.\n\n\nToday, Studio-visible component metadata is registered in\n`feedbax.web.services.component_registry.ComponentRegistry`, while instantiation from\n`GraphSpec` is still handled by hardcoded dispatch in\n`feedbax.web.serialization.spec_to_graph()`.\n\nThis works, but it creates drift:\n\n- A component can appear in Studio without a complete executable builder.\n- A component can be executable without a durable public type contract.\n- Agents cannot reliably ask Feedbax what component types exist or how to instantiate\n  them.\n- Project-specific components lack a principled promotion path from sketch to reviewed\n  code.\n\n\nUse one logical registry contract for built-in and external components. Built-in\ncomponents can remain in normal Feedbax source files; they do not need to be moved into a\nspecial registry directory. What should change is that built-ins and extensions should be\ndescribed through the same definition format.\n\nA component registry entry should include:\n\n- Stable type ID, such as `feedbax.Gain` or `rlrmp.GoCueGate`.\n- Display name, category, description, and Studio icon hints.\n- Provider/package identity and version.\n- Python import path and/or builder reference.\n- Input and output ports.\n- Port type hints and shape/dtype constraints where available.\n- Parameter schema, defaults, and validation rules.\n- State schema or state-view metadata when relevant.\n- Serialization/deserialization hooks.\n- Compatibility predicates, such as supported graph contexts or backends.\n- Whether the component is a leaf implementation or a composite graph template.\n- Provenance/review metadata for externally supplied or agent-created components.\n\n\nLeaf components are executable Python code. If an agent or project needs a new atomic\ncomputation, it should provide a real `Component` subclass or builder with JAX/Equinox\ncompatible implementation, tests, metadata, and serialization behavior.\n\nLeaf component code should generally live in a reviewed Python package, not an anonymous\nloose file. For example, a project package could expose components with an entry point:\n\n```toml\n[project.entry-points.\"feedbax.components\"]\nrlrmp = \"rlrmp.feedbax_components:register_components\"\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```python\ndef register_components(registry):\n    registry.register(...)\n```\n\nThis gives the component a stable import path, versioning, tests, source control, and\nprovenance. A loose local module path can remain useful for prototypes, but durable run\nmanifests should not depend on unreviewed local files without explicit provenance and\nwarnings.\n\n\nComposite components can be graph templates built from existing registered components.\nThey may not require new Python leaf code if the graph template fully describes their\nbehavior. The registry entry should mark them as composite and include or reference a\ntemplate `GraphSpec`.\n\nStudio can use this to show high-level constructs while Feedbax still lowers them into\nnormal graphs for execution.\n\n\nFeedbax has several things that can look like \"subgraphs\" from the UI or modeling point\nof view, but they should not all be treated the same:\n\n- **Causal Feedbax subgraphs**: ordinary nested `Graph` values. In runtime terms,\n  `Graph` is itself a `Component`, so a subgraph can appear as a node inside another\n  graph. In `GraphSpec`, nested graphs are represented through `subgraphs` keyed by the\n  containing node name; `spec_to_graph()` can recursively instantiate a `Subgraph`.\n- **Template/composite components**: menu entries that expand to a reusable `GraphSpec`.\n  These are not leaf computations. Their registry entry should include\n  `is_composite=True`, a `template_graph`, and, when needed, `template_ui_state`.\n- **Lowered model templates**: high-level graph templates that are eventually lowered\n  into ordinary Feedbax graphs or specialized components. These should declare both\n  their template contract and their lowering/instantiation behavior.\n- **Penzai subgraphs**: currently represented as a `PenzaiSubgraph`/`PenzaiAdapter`\n  wrapper. From the Feedbax graph's perspective this behaves more like a leaf component\n  with a registered builder and port mapping, even though internally it may contain a\n  Penzai model graph.\n- **Acausal or structured-equation subgraphs**: construction-time equation systems that\n  are assembled into a `DAEComponent`/`AcausalSystem`. From the causal Feedbax graph's\n  perspective the assembled system behaves like a component, but its internal equation\n  elements may deserve their own registry if Studio begins editing them directly.\n\nThe registry should therefore distinguish at least:\n\n- `leaf`: executable `Component` subclass or builder.\n- `causal_subgraph`: nested Feedbax `Graph` with explicit ports and bindings.\n- `composite_template`: reusable graph template that expands into a `GraphSpec`.\n- `external_subgraph`: wrapper around another graph framework, such as Penzai.\n- `structured_equation_system`: acausal/equation descriptor assembled into a causal\n  component.\n\nThis distinction matters for validation and manifests. A durable graph spec should record\nwhether a node is a leaf, a nested graph, a template expansion, an external wrapped\nsubgraph, or an assembled equation system, because each has different reproducibility and\nlowering requirements.\n\n\nThe high-level registry contract is shared: every entry needs a stable type ID, display\nmetadata, parameters, ports, validation, serialization, compatibility, and provenance.\nThe executable/reproducibility contract differs by kind:\n\n| Kind | Primary payload | Code requirement | Validation requirement | Manifest requirement |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `leaf` | Python class or builder | Required; must resolve to importable reviewed code | Validate parameters, ports, state/init behavior, JAX/Equinox compatibility | Record type ID, import path/builder, version, parameter values, and code provenance |\n| `causal_subgraph` | `GraphSpec` plus ports/bindings | Not required if all child nodes resolve; optional builder can generate the spec | Validate child nodes, wires, ports, bindings, cycles, shape/type hints, and child registries | Record nested `GraphSpec`, child type IDs, expansion provenance, and any template/builder that generated it |\n| `composite_template` | Reusable template `GraphSpec` and optional UI state | Not required for static templates; required only if template is generated by code | Validate template parameters, expansion result, exposed ports, and that all expanded children resolve | Record template type ID, template version/hash, parameters, and expanded graph or a resolvable template reference |\n| `external_subgraph` | Wrapper spec for another graph/model framework | Required wrapper/builder; external payload may be serialized or imported | Validate wrapper ports, external dependency availability, payload schema/hash, and state binding | Record wrapper type ID, external framework/version, builder/import path, payload reference/hash, and port mappings |\n| `structured_equation_system` | Equation/element/connection spec lowered to a component | Required assembler/lowering implementation; individual elements may be registered specs | Validate equation elements, domains, ports, connection constraints, parameters, solvability/lowering checks | Record equation-system spec, element type IDs, assembler version, lowered component type, and lowering provenance |\n\nFor a project or agent creating a new reusable \"component\", the first question should be\nwhich payload is actually being contributed:\n\n- New atomic computation: register a `leaf`.\n- New reusable arrangement of existing Feedbax nodes: register a `composite_template` or\n  a saved `causal_subgraph`.\n- New generated graph family: register a `composite_template` with a builder/lowering\n  function, and record generated expansions in manifests.\n- New Penzai-style wrapped model family: register an `external_subgraph` wrapper and its\n  payload/port-mapping contract.\n- New equation-domain primitive or assembled physical system: register structured\n  equation elements and/or a `structured_equation_system` lowering contract.\n\nThe uncertain part is how fine-grained the structured-equation registry should become.\nIf Studio eventually edits acausal internals directly, equation elements, domains,\nconnectors, and assembled systems probably need separate registry entries. If structured\nequation systems remain authored in Python and only exposed as assembled components, a\nsingle `structured_equation_system` entry may be enough for the first implementation.\n\nSimilarly, static causal subgraphs and composite templates may collapse to the same\nimplementation initially. The conceptual distinction is still useful: a causal subgraph\nis an actual nested graph in a model spec, while a composite template is a reusable\npalette/catalog item that expands into such a graph.\n\n\nBuilt-in Feedbax components should use the same logical registry as project extensions,\nbut they can remain ordinary Feedbax source code. This avoids a needless source-layout\nrefactor while still giving agents, Studio, manifests, and deserialization one canonical\ncatalog.\n\n\nAgent-created leaf components should follow a review lifecycle:\n\n1. Draft code and metadata in a project package or quarantined proposal location.\n2. Validate importability, JAX compatibility, parameter schema, ports, and serialization.\n3. Run focused tests.\n4. Record provenance: issue, session, commit, author/agent, and review status.\n5. Promote to a stable package/module path before use in durable run manifests.\n\nIf a prototype component is used before promotion, the run manifest should record that it\ndepends on unreviewed local code and include enough provenance to reconstruct or reject\nthe run later.\n\n\nThe registry can be generalized incrementally:\n\n1. Add a registry entry type that includes both metadata and a builder reference.\n2. Register current built-ins through that entry type.\n3. Teach `spec_to_graph()` to consult the registry before falling back to hardcoded\n   dispatch.\n4. Move hardcoded builders behind registry entries over time.\n5. Add project package entry-point discovery.\n6. Add validation/reporting for components visible in Studio but not executable.\n\nThis avoids a large break while moving toward a principled model.\n\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}